1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,941
Time for a different kind of news.

2
00:00:02,941 --> 00:00:07,967
Two festivals happening right now
in Korea. One has jazz on an island.

3
00:00:07,967 --> 00:00:11,700
The other has movies in
Korea's second-largest city.

4
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:14,290
I'm in. Both. Where do I sign?

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:18,320
To help us plan, our travel
expert, Professor Indoor.

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:19,630
I've been to both.

7
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:20,860
Physically?

8
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:25,050
...Emotionally. I watched
a lot of street view.

9
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,040
We'll take it. Let's start with the jazz.

10
00:00:28,390 --> 00:00:31,252
The Jarasum International Jazz Festival.

11
00:00:31,252 --> 00:00:34,042
October ninth to eleventh, in Gapyeong,

12
00:00:34,042 --> 00:00:37,190
about seventy kilometers
northeast of Seoul.

13
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:40,500
Jarasum. That sounds like a dinosaur.

14
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:42,413
It's not a dinosaur.

15
00:00:42,413 --> 00:00:47,570
"Jarasum" means "terrapin island."
A terrapin is a kind of turtle.

16
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:52,880
A jazz festival on Turtle
Island. So the tempo is... slow?

17
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:58,908
Technically, it's a group of four small
islands in the Bukhan River: East,

18
00:00:58,908 --> 00:01:00,750
West, Middle, and South.

19
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:02,727
East, West, Middle, South.

20
00:01:02,727 --> 00:01:05,980
Whoever named those islands
did it on a lunch break.

21
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:08,288
It started in two thousand four,

22
00:01:08,288 --> 00:01:11,530
with thirty teams of musicians
from twelve countries.

23
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:12,977
Two thousand four.

24
00:01:12,977 --> 00:01:17,000
Back then my biggest problem was
burning a CD without it skipping.

25
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:21,776
This year is the twenty-third edition.
And the government has noticed:

26
00:01:21,776 --> 00:01:27,910
it was named a "Representative Festival of Korea"
in twenty sixteen and again in twenty eighteen.

27
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,580
So it got a gold star. Twice.

28
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:35,404
In twenty twenty-three, it
joined the Europe Jazz Network.

29
00:01:35,404 --> 00:01:39,339
And each year it puts one
country in the spotlight.

30
00:01:39,339 --> 00:01:41,730
Poland, then Hungary last year.

31
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:44,640
Poland, Hungary... and this year?

32
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:45,537
France.

33
00:01:45,537 --> 00:01:51,550
It marks one hundred forty years of
diplomatic ties between Korea and France.

34
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:56,700
One hundred forty years. I can't
keep a houseplant alive for one.

35
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,158
The poster is by a French
illustrator, Agathe Briot.

36
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:03,370
Two dancers, a lot of color.

37
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,080
Okay, nice poster. But
who's actually playing?

38
00:02:07,430 --> 00:02:12,075
Headliners include drummer Nate
Smith. He won two Grammys this year,

39
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:16,310
and this is his first time back
at Jarasum in seventeen years.

40
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,740
Seventeen years. That's the
longest rest in music history.

41
00:02:21,090 --> 00:02:25,048
Cuban pianist Alfredo
Rodriguez plays Saturday.

42
00:02:25,048 --> 00:02:30,690
And Armenian pianist Tigran Hamasyan
closes the festival on Sunday.

43
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:36,720
Closing on a piano. Smooth. Now,
how do I get to a turtle island?

44
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:42,464
The ITX train from Yongsan to Gapyeong
takes about fifty-five minutes.

45
00:02:42,464 --> 00:02:47,550
Then it's a twenty-minute walk
from the station, or a shuttle bus.

46
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:51,100
I've walked that route. ...On a map.

47
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:53,558
Tickets are sold through Yes24.

48
00:02:53,558 --> 00:02:56,958
Kids in elementary school
and younger get in free.

49
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:59,610
Middle and high schoolers get half off.

50
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,825
And it lands on the
Hangul Day long weekend.

51
00:03:02,825 --> 00:03:05,560
Everyone's bringing a blanket and a snack.

52
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:09,605
The whole festival is outdoors,
with seating on the grass.

53
00:03:09,605 --> 00:03:12,790
It's mid-October, so the
leaves are at their peak.

54
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:18,580
Blanket, snacks, a Grammy winner, a
turtle island. Ten out of ten weekend.

55
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:23,696
But if you like your art
with subtitles, head south.

56
00:03:23,696 --> 00:03:28,350
The Busan International Film
Festival, October sixth to fifteenth.

57
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:32,380
BIFF. Like Biff from Back
to the Future? The bully?

58
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:33,653
No relation.

59
00:03:33,653 --> 00:03:38,730
It used to be called PIFF, with a
P, from the old spelling, Pusan.

60
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:43,240
One letter. Somebody had
to reprint every T-shirt.

61
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:45,645
It began in nineteen ninety-six,

62
00:03:45,645 --> 00:03:49,563
with a hundred seventy-three
films from thirty-one countries.

63
00:03:49,563 --> 00:03:52,710
About a hundred eighty-four
thousand people came.

64
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,740
That's a lot of popcorn.

65
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:58,912
It was the first international
film festival in Korea.

66
00:03:58,912 --> 00:04:04,290
The opening film was "Secrets and Lies,"
by the British director Mike Leigh.

67
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:09,120
A British family drama to open a
festival in Korea. That's range.

68
00:04:09,470 --> 00:04:14,671
In twenty eleven, it moved to its
permanent home, the Busan Cinema Center,

69
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:16,990
in a district called Centum City.

70
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:21,260
A permanent home. For a festival,
that's basically a mortgage.

71
00:04:21,610 --> 00:04:25,718
In twenty fourteen, Busan was
named a UNESCO City of Film.

72
00:04:25,718 --> 00:04:28,693
And in twenty twenty, during the pandemic,

73
00:04:28,693 --> 00:04:35,210
the festival showed a hundred ninety-two films
to a total audience of about twenty thousand.

74
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:38,760
Twenty thousand. The popcorn
must have been lonely.

75
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:41,512
Last year, for the thirtieth edition,

76
00:04:41,512 --> 00:04:45,018
it moved to September and
added a competition section.

77
00:04:45,018 --> 00:04:48,070
The Grand Prize winner
now closes the festival.

78
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:50,688
So the festival ends with the winner.

79
00:04:50,688 --> 00:04:53,140
A festival with a built-in happy ending.

80
00:04:53,490 --> 00:04:55,702
This year, it's back in October.

81
00:04:55,702 --> 00:04:59,434
Three hundred sixteen films
from fifty-nine countries,

82
00:04:59,434 --> 00:05:02,130
including ninety-three world premieres.

83
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:05,268
Three hundred sixteen films in ten days.

84
00:05:05,268 --> 00:05:09,520
That's more than thirty films
a day. Even I can't binge that.

85
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:14,360
The opening ceremony was on October
sixth, at the outdoor theater.

86
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:18,510
Michelle Yeoh received the Asian
Filmmaker of the Year award.

87
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:23,980
And there's a special program called
"Everything Everywhere All for Michelle."

88
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:26,564
Everything Everywhere All for Michelle.

89
00:05:26,564 --> 00:05:28,970
That's the best title I've heard all week.

90
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:33,633
The competition jury is presided
over by the director Zhang Yimou.

91
00:05:33,633 --> 00:05:37,880
And among the guests are Alfonso
Cuaron and Charlotte Gainsbourg.

92
00:05:38,230 --> 00:05:42,131
There's also a program called
"Those Beautiful Years,

93
00:05:42,131 --> 00:05:45,590
Ahn Sung-ki," with six
films of the late actor.

94
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:47,860
That's a lovely way to remember him.

95
00:05:48,210 --> 00:05:52,719
Some screenings are outdoors,
at the Open Cinema. In October.

96
00:05:52,719 --> 00:05:54,050
So bring a jacket.

97
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:58,720
A movie under the sky, in a jacket.
That's my kind of date night.

98
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:02,377
Tickets are open to everyone,
not just industry people.

99
00:06:02,377 --> 00:06:04,723
You book on the festival's ticket site,

100
00:06:04,723 --> 00:06:08,030
and leftover seats may be
sold at ticket boxes on site.

101
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:12,460
I once booked a ticket to a movie
about travel. Does that count?

102
00:06:12,810 --> 00:06:17,450
No. Busan is about two and a
half hours from Seoul by KTX.

103
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:22,642
So your weekend is jazz on a turtle
island, or films in a port city.

104
00:06:22,642 --> 00:06:24,920
Or both, if you have a jet pack.

105
00:06:25,270 --> 00:06:29,750
Dates can change, so check each
festival's official site before you go.

106
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,180
Core, which one are you going to?

107
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:38,290
I will be at home. With a spreadsheet.
That was your Korea events guide.

108
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:39,890
Thanks for watching.
